 "version": 1,
 "note": "Emotion director table. Single source of truth for poses; the mind only knows family ids. Every ladder pose must be group a or a* in the groups sheet and live in the face catalogue (unit tests). Pip-clause lexicon: lowercase ASCII words; one word may offer alternatives with |; kinds: word (one word anywhere), phrase (consecutive words anywhere), start (words begin the clause, no leading quote), whole (the whole clause, one trailing .!? allowed). Every phrase is tight by default (no comma between its words); a row opts out with tight false. Optional row attributes: after break (the match must end the clause or be followed by . , ! ? ; : or a dash or ellipsis); or_next (with after break: phrases that may follow the match directly instead of a break, a closed whitelist); after end (the match must END the clause, optionally followed by one comma and one name or pronoun); opener true (a weak greeting: dropped when any other row matches); refuse_next (phrases that must not follow the match, directly or after a comma); max_rest (at most this many words after the match). Optional rest (a closed whitelist, replaces after): the match must end the clause (closing punctuation, or one break and a name), or one of these phrases must follow it, after a space or a break, and end the clause; anything else is silence; the glad, happy, pleased and good news rows use it. Apology rows also take back a negator right after the match, or in front of the match's own last word again (sorry, not sorry). Positive and neutral rows refuse a sense verb or a body or hardware noun (body_words, minus the greeting idioms in body_idioms that end the clause) anywhere in the clause outside the match: no sense or body claim raises a pose. Context guards for every row: a hyphenated compound, and a one or two letter match in capitals, never match; quote marks around the match, and any clause that reports or defines words (said, says, the word, called, means, spelled, stands for ...), never read; every row refuses a question (the sentence ends with ?); word and phrase rows refuse a negator or hedge (not, no, never, if, would, could, wish, hope, suppose, perhaps, maybe, think, say ...) within four tokens before them in the same sentence, a guard phrase (far from, less than, anything but) just before, and a sentence that opens with if, would, could, might, whether or in case. Rows of a family with polarity 0 or above also refuse a clause that carries a caution word or a negator outside the match (unless the row sets cautions false, as the comforting support rows do). All matches in a clause are collected: a contrast word anywhere, or two matches of different polarity, give no reaction; any other match beats an opener. Families may set wire_min, the lowest strength a heard cue needs.",
